On 9 Sivan 5761, Arne Vajh�j wrote:

> Question: as far as I know, then GNU Emacs uses Lisp, and GNU Emacs is
> ported to VMS, will that not imply that GNU Lisp is already ported to
> VMS ?

Being an Emacs aficionado of sorts, perhaps I can help:

1.  Emacs hasn't compiled without massive changes in some time (since
    whatever compiler came at the same time as VMS 5.5, from what I've
    heard; I haven't succeeded with C V5.6 or 5.7) on VMS.

2.  When it did compile, the latest version I heard that worked was
    19.28; since then v19 has revved to .34, v20 came out with seven
    releases, and RSN we'll see v21.  These are big improvements over
    19 (in my opinion; others will say otherwise for various good
    reasons) and it would be great if these could be ported to VMS.  I
    have heard rumors in this newsgroup that somebody is working on
    Emacs 20 for VMS but I haven't heard anything else.

3.  Most people referring to Lisp these days are either referring to
    Common Lisp or Scheme (and if you want to start a flamewar, go to
    comp.lang.lisp and say that Scheme is a Lisp); Emacs Lisp is pretty
    far from Common Lisp in some important areas, and development
    towards replacing Emacs Lisp with Scheme is not currently
    progressing as far as I can tell.

4.  "GNU Lisp" would probably be GCL (which seemed to me like it had
    stagnated some time ago, but I haven't kept up lately).  There is
    also CLISP, an interpreter (which might actually be portable to VMS,
    at least as much so as any other mostly-command-line GNU tool) and
    CMUCL (which is extremely difficult to port; it has to be
    bootstrapped - I don't see this showing up on VMS any time soon).

I found a working binary of Emacs v18 without networking code, which is
OK for some editing, but I would love to see either a working binary of
Emacs 19 with support for UCX, or even better, a recent port of Emacs.
I would probably do everything on VMS if Emacs were there; as it is I
keep a Linux box handy for email and news (like this).

(For those who are thinking, "Isn't Emacs just an editor?" - let me
explain.  Emacs is an editor similar to TPU, except that its extension
language is a dialect of Lisp, and recent versions have networking
capabilities - which means that you could write a network client in your
editor.  This works surprisingly well; I do all my email and news in
Emacs, and have been able to customize it to the nth degree as a result.
XEmacs is a fork in Emacs development which introduced things like
syntax-based color coding in non-X11 editing and image-viewing and
proportional-font support in X11 sessions; Emacs 21 will catch up in
these areas when it comes out.  This means you could have a web browser
built into your editor - and indeed one has been available for some
time, that supports frames, tables, etc.  Yes, interpreted Lisp is
slower than native machine code much of the time, but it works
surprisingly well and is very easy to change on the fly - and it can
easily be made to emulate other environments, like ELSE (Emacs LSE,
apparently compatible with LSE's language description files), VIper (for
masochists who like that vi sort of thing), and so on.)
