Jonathan Thornburg wrote:
> 
> In article <9ejpfu$l02$1@ausnews.austin.ibm.com>,
> McCalpin <mccalpin@austin.ibm.com> wrote:
> >Partial writes are ugly for many reasons:
> >
> >1. They may require lots of partial writes if (as is most
> >   reasonable) the merge function in the memory can only
> >   write contiguous fields.
> >   For example, a 128 Byte store buffer that has every
> >   other byte written either needs the memory controller
> >   merge function to support byte masks (yechh!) or it
> >   needs to make 64 partial writes (double yechh!).
> 
> Hmm.  A naive programmer's view would say
> * write 64 individual non-contiguous bytes
> * write-through cache --> writes go to main memory
> * expect 64 write transactions to memory
> which is just about what happens.

The off-chip secondary cache did not have to be write-though for
the 21064 (EV4) and likely wasn't in most implementations.  In
the 21164 (EV5), the 1st level cache is write-through but the
on-chip second level cache is write-back.

-eric




