Steve,

The DECpc XL is the immediate predecessor of the Celebris XL.  They
use the same basic chassis and power supply.  AFAIK, there is not an
electronic manual for the DECpc XL, but you can download the one for
the Celebris XL from the following URL.  The differences between the
two models of computers are the main logic board (known as motherboard
in the non-DEC world), the processor card, and the plastic front
panel.  Otherwise, no difference.

http://www4.service.digital.com/documentation/pcdoc/celxl5.htm

With the DECpc XL main logic board, you can install any 5v Intel 486
processor or the Intel Pentium OverDrive, preferably 83MHz, for sure.


Some versions of DECpc XL main logic board support low end Pentium
CPUs, up to 133MHz, with a different CPU card.  Some do not.  Let me
know the DEC part number of the main logic board, and I can give you
more exact information.  The part number is inscribed on the board.
If you open up the chassis, place it on its side with the opening
facing up and the front panel facing you, the part number can be found
etched on the left edge of the board.  It is either 50-nnnnn or
54-nnnnn.

All versions of DECpc XL support the Pentium 60 or 66, with a
different CPU card.  This is not worth the effort.

If you want to replace the main logic board with the latest 54-23606
Celebris XL board and companion 54-24490 processor card, you can run
either a single 200MHz Pentium or dual 166MHz Pentium CPUs, the latter
with an operating system that supports them, i.e. Windows NT, Windows
2000 or Linux.

I have all these parts in stock along with processors, but it's likely
that the cost of shipping to NZ added to the sale price may be more
than you would want to pay... Ben Myers

