David desJardins <desj@math.berkeley.edu> writes:

> That turns out not to be the case; in fact, write-allocate can cost more
> than a factor of two.  A write-allocate version of the 21064, which
> would have to use two bus cycles to read each cache line and then two
> more bus cycles to write the cache line, could easily be more than twice
> as slow as the actual 21064, which can actually write a partial cache
> line in only one bus cycle.

The merging has to occur somewhere. Given that ECC typically covers more than
32 bits - that was the 21064's basic unit - merging always costs, either in
the processor or in the memory system.

With the facts on the 21064 as presented in this thread, I would venture to
guess that it always used 32-bit ECCs (coming from DEC, I cannot imagine they
built a system without ECC). As that is the lowest size that supports a useful
ECC, doing byte-wise merging without substantial, and in essence equivalent to
write-allocate, costs in the memory subsystem seems impoassible (e.g., for the
21264A and later which support the 16- and 8-bit loads/stores).

	Jan


