At this point in time - probably true.  "Too little, too late."  Not a very
appealing mantra, but the only one we've heard in the last 10 years from 
Digipaq.

But at while it might be foolish, it would at least represent action.
Compaq's current slow as a tortoise movements will likely seem horrifically
foolhardy in 4 years when AMD or Intel has won a decisive victory in the 64
bit space and 90% of the whole computer market (including especially the
high performance market) is running on one or the other family of chips. 
That is, having failed to position the Alpha preemptively to keep Intel and
AMD out of the 64 bit market, and having failed to position their OS's to
be able to capitalize on the economies of scale that would accrue from
being able to run on either AMD or Intel's 64 bit chips, Compaq could well
find itself with a high cost, behind the curve processor, and no products
of their own to run on the new platforms.  (Other than Compaq fortran.)
Once again Compaq will be just another PC vendor selling somebody else's
CPU and somebody else's OS - and watching all the profits go to somebody 
else.

By the way, my money's on AMD.  The real x86 backwards compatibility built
into the Hammer should let it run existing apps while simultaneously adding 
new 64 bit ones.  Conversely, the Itanic's 32 bit performance is so bad
that that strategy won't work there.  And since software availability is
half the battle, the upgrade path to Hammer should be easier than the one
to Itanic. 

