The VAX 4000 model 200 is a slow machine, in comparison to a model 600 or
650 or so.

The early production systems had a cache problem which caused them to
corrupt the OS or fail to properly operate such as not deciphering the
password or even not recognizing the username at login. The problem was
attributed to a cache design that had been set up with redundancy in it's
register set to increase chip yeaild and when the chip was tested the bad
cache registers were to be wired out and the good ones wired in, but the
wrong registers got wired out and the wrong registers got wired in.

Most of the CPU modules as I remember were successfully replaced by Field
Service and VMS was modified to turn off all the cahce in the machine if it
encountered a problem so that at least there was a fail safe. After all, at
the time, you wouldn't want to be doing life threatening calculations with a
machine that could get the wrong answer.

Apparently today, these types of considerations don't evencome into play. I
see the ubiquitous PC being used all over the place in perfoming life
threatening calculations, without any fail safes or guarentees.

