"John D. McCalpin, Ph.D." <mccalpin@gmp246.austin.ibm.com> writes:
> The latter approach requires partial line writes to memory in cases
> where the buffer is flushed without being completely filled.  I
> consider partial line writes to memory to be exceedingly ugly.

The 21064 used a mask to write only the data that's actually in the
write buffer.  I don't know why this is "ugly".  Also, since it had a
128-bit bus and 32-byte cache lines, if all of the data to be written
happened to be in the first 16 bytes, it could truncate the protocol
before the second bus cycle to save time.  I wrote some code that used
only memory locations congruent to {0,1} mod 4, to take advantage of this.

