In article <3B0D56B1.A91BE4A1@sgi.com>, Eric C. Fromm <efromm@sgi.com> wrote:
>McCalpin wrote:
>> 
>> The Alpha 21064 (EV4) processor has a write-through,
>> non-allocating Data cache, supported by four line-sized
>> write buffers.
>> 
>> So if a store misses in the cache, the line is not read
>> into the cache, and the stores are gathered into the
>> store buffer.
>> 
>> There are two obvious ways to maintain coherency here:
>> 1. allocate the line into the store buffer and update
>>    the single master copy there, or
>> 2. merge the contents of the store buffer with memory
>>    when it comes time to flush the store buffer.
>> 
>> The latter approach requires partial line writes to
>> memory in cases where the buffer is flushed without
>> being completely filled.  I consider partial line writes
>> to memory to be exceedingly ugly.
>
>I can't answer with certainty. Unfortunately, I tossed my
>EV4 docs recently. But I'm fairly certain partial writes
>was the solution. On the other hand, what is it about
>partial writes that seems so ugly in this context?

Partial writes are ugly for many reasons:

1. They may require lots of partial writes if (as is most
   reasonable) the merge function in the memory can only
   write contiguous fields.
   For example, a 128 Byte store buffer that has every
   other byte written either needs the memory controller
   merge function to support byte masks (yechh!) or it
   needs to make 64 partial writes (double yechh!).

2. Unmerged data is really ugly in MP machines.  If a 
   store buffer is partially filled and generates a snoop
   hit for another processor's load, you probably need 
   to do the partial writes back to memory, merge the data
   there, and then satisfy the load from memory.  This 
   makes interventions really slow.  You probably can't
   give the partial data to the other process, since its
   data cache does not support byte enables.  You could
   (in theory) transfer the data with the byte enables
   to the other processor's store buffer, but this is also
   ugly, since it would likely evict a real store that was
   trying to gather there.  This would also be a bad idea
   if the load was due to an Icache miss, since the Icache
   generally has to take the data in a shared state.
   There are a couple of extra possibilities, I suppose....
   The processor executing the load could get data from both
   memory and the processor that had it dirty, and could do
   the merge itself.  Or the source processor could allocate
   the data, do the merge, and service the load.  

   All of these approaches are exceedingly complex compared
   to the simple approach of loading the data from memory
   into the store buffer and maintaining the master copy
   of all of the bytes in the line in the store buffer.
-- 
