"John D. McCalpin, Ph.D." <mccalpin@austin.ibm.com> writes:
> Not correct.  The 21064 does memory I/O in 32 Byte cache lines.
> Within the cache line, loads and stores operate on 8 Byte quantities,

This is exactly the same as my supposedly "not correct" statement.  As I
said, your putative example can't occur, because it's not possible for
some bytes in a word to be modified and not others.

> "Disastrously slow" connotes slowdowns of orders of magnitude, 
> while write allocation is unable to provide a slowdown of more than
> a factor of two on even the most perverse applications.

That turns out not to be the case; in fact, write-allocate can cost more
than a factor of two.  A write-allocate version of the 21064, which
would have to use two bus cycles to read each cache line and then two
more bus cycles to write the cache line, could easily be more than twice
as slow as the actual 21064, which can actually write a partial cache
line in only one bus cycle.  Write-allocate also makes it much more
likely that the processor will stall, by increasing the number of
outstanding memory accesses, unless additional hardware is added.

But a factor of two is bad enough for me.  You can concentrate on
creating bigger disasters, if you choose.

>> Fast beats simple any day.
> 
> This is demonstrably incorrect.  For example, Alpha processors have
> had performance at or near the leading edge for most of the past five
> years or so, and yet Compaq only has about a 22% market share in the
> HPC market (CY2000 results as reported by IDC).

The reason for that is certainly not that the competing processors are
simpler!  The Alpha processors are both faster and simpler than their
competitors.

