Dennis,

You have several options, though for simplicity reasons and to support the
most recent SRM console firmware, you'll want to buy a 164LX with either the
stock 2MB or 4MB cache options.  The board should cost you about $200-$300
out on the net. Ebay is one option, Island Computers (a vendor) is another.
Microway will charge you upwards of $1300.00 for a motherboard/CPU combo and
seem highly reluctant to sell just boards (they probably have a stock of old
164LX kits which include a CPU of either 500-600 MHz in the box.)

You can occasionally find replacement boards for the Personal Workstation's
on the net as well, these are modifications of the 164LX with the addition
of some PCI slots (dual PCI buses and 5 slots, unlike LX with only one PCI
bus/4 slots), DIMM sockets (6 sockets instead of 4) and DIP switches instead
of jumpers and a COAST module for the cache SIMM instead of
non-upgradeable/soldered on cache as with 164LX/164SX/164UX/164BX and the
old PC164 (the Alcor II rather than the Pyxis system chipset).

All are ATX 2.0 compliant and use standard cases and power supplies. Don't
build a system with less than a 300 watt supply; the motherboard/CPU alone
use 100 watts of power at 533Mhz and better than 110 watts at 667MHz. Each
DIMM is another 1-2 watts, the hard drives are up to 30 watts max for the
15K RPM drives (when fully operational).

Buy some cooling fans to keep the case cool (pcpowerandcooling.com) with at
least one inlet fan and at least another exhaust fan.  The power supply fan
is not sufficient to properly cool the case.  Even the Personal Workstation
cases can use some help at 600MHz or faster (you can clock the system up to
667MHz with a little effort).

Good luck,



