In article <qh3d9ypm8w.fsf@ruckus.brouhaha.com>,
Eric Smith  <eric-no-spam-for-me@brouhaha.com> wrote:
>Paul Repacholi <prep@prep.synonet.com> writes about the PDP-11/60
>> Worse, originally, it had ID and 22-bit memory.
>
>Originally, as in a marketing requirements document, or originally
>as in something that engineering actually designed?  The 11/60
>print set doesn't appear to show anything that would be usable
>for I&D support or for 22-bit.
>

Actually there was an 11/68 on the drawing board at DEC as a follow on
to the 11/74 which was basically an SMP 11/60 type box with 22 bit
addressing and separate I&D...

This box was called the bluefish (or bluefin, I think).

There also was the PDP11 that had the 11/780 VAX SBI (synchronous
backplane interconnect on it called the Unicorn... This was before 
the 11/780.   Early Vax prototypes using the MS780A had reference docs
to the Unicorn and Unicorn Memory.

There was a great notes file on never-built or prototype PDP11's
at DEC... As a history major in college I always wanted to write a book
on the subject of undocumented DEC history -- but the folks who were
there then are disapearing from site far too quickly.

I wish there was some kind of on-line DEC history web site where
information could be contributed and some urban legends confirmed or
debunked for once and for all.

Bill

