In article <f0iO6.11316$5X4.2923506@typhoon.ne.mediaone.net>, "Terry C. Shannon" <terryshannon@mediaone.net> writes:
>
>We simply are not going to see a $995 VMS workstation. 

That's a pity because the price point has moved on again.  When I did the 
PCVMS survey $2500 was price competitive, it is not now.  If the Q finally 
did manage to ship a $2500 machine it would be once more at the 3X price 
and 20% more performance (and the second value is doubtful, given the speed 
increases in the AMD and Intel lines.)  For once I'd like to see Compaq
offer VMS with purchase price *value*.  (Digital did this for a while with 
the VAX in the early 80's, which is the primary reason they did so well in 
then, and Compaq does so poorly now.)  I say this having just put together
a machine based on a Sun netra motherboard which is sort of a souped up
version of the Blade 100.  Other than the motherboard it is literally just
commodity PC components (good ones, but commodity).  The whole thing cost
$2300, is a server with no user limitations, and has a 75 Gb IBM ATA disk,
DVD drive, and 1 Gb of RAM.  (No graphics card in there, as there was a
delivery problem with the one card that would have worked and I needed to
get this machine on line.  That card, by the way, was only $160 or so and
included sound as well.)   It outperforms the DS10 it replaces by a mile on
disk IO with our applications and since most of our apps are strictly
limited by that, it is actually faster overall than the DS10.  (But that is
a VMS slow IO issue - not a hardware one, other than the cruddy ATA33 in
the DS10 vs. the ATA66 on the netra card.) 

The point is, Sun can build this, Sun is building this, and evidently, Sun
is selling them, at least to certain disgruntled ex-Compaq customers. 

>within Compaq who realize that a Samsung UP1500-based system with 128MB of
>memory, a 10GB disk, an entry 2D graphics card, and a VMS or Tru64 license
>could be built and profitably sold for $2995. 

And the customers ignoring it in droves and buying instead $1000 Athlons and 
running Linux on them - which will be nearly as fast and 1/3 the price.
Moreover, Compaq seems to have picked the wrong horse in the memory race 
and that built in Rambus controller may very well mean that memory for 
Alphas will once again cost more than memory for the competition.  That 1Gb
of memory in my new machine is registered PC133 direct from crucial.com -
having to buy that much memory from Sun (or Compaq) would have been 
completely out of the question.

Compaq cannot compete against commodity hardware on price, and more and 
more so, they can barely compete on performance.  Perhaps they should just 
accept the inevitable and deal with AMD, trading them Alpha technology for
key design changes in AMDs forthcoming chips that would allow VMS, Tru64, 
and Tandem to use those parts - and then port like mad.  The net effect
should be that it would allow at least the first two OS's to run on
commodity systems which would drive the hardware cost for both down into
the range where they could compete toe to toe with WNT, W2k, WinXP etc. 
And they could still build big machines out of the AMD CPUs and charge the
high margins there. Probably they'd come out way ahead by not having to
spend money on their proprietary chip. 

Regards,

David Mathog
mathog@caltech.edu
Manager, sequence analysis facility, biology division, Caltech 


