Yes indeed. A very positive development. Recall that when Rich took over
VMS, it was a business in decline. Aided by their familiar, a Gartner
emigre, Decpaq was doing its damnest to migrate customers to Windows. Unlike
many a pointy haired Senior Manager (including the then-CTO), Rich realized
that the claims of near-term NT-VMS functional parity were so much Microsoft
codswallop. Rich managed to restore credibility with the customer base while
returning VMS to modest growth mode.

Not an insignificant accomplishment.

So Rich now has the whole Alpha-VMS-Tru64 franchise. If he can repeat his
VMSuccess with the High Performance Systems franchise, things are bound to
improve in the Alpha space.

Mark is a VMS Group emigre who spent the last 18 months in the Services
world. Since a goodly number of VMS engagements are services-led, Mark has
the right background to exploit both the OS and Services elements of the
equation. All in all, this looks like a pretty good deal.

And that's about all I can contribute by way of pontification and
expounding, other than to say that VMS Groupies and customers alike have
responded quite positively to the latest round of Management Musical Chairs.





