>the "Ma Bell" days, no such feature actually was available, at least not
>"officially"). Too bad a similar scenario in today's broadband world
>would be such a challenge.

I have no real problem with her using it to communicate with her friends
but there has to be a limit.  My wife and I have asked her to quit at a
decent hour but she has abused it several times after we have long since
retired to the bedroom.  The SNMP request now merely enforces our control
over her usage.  As for the phone, I prefer her IMing her friends.  I do
not appreciate the telephone ringing after hours when I want my quiet and
personal time and I imagine that other parents feel the same.  My daughter
is NOT permitted to call past 8:00pm with few exceptions.  Unfortunately,
other parents do not exercise and enforce the same limitations.

Some who attended CETS may recall my dissertation to a small group gather-
ing in one of the hallways on my "technical" solution to the telephone use
problem in my home.  It's amasing how well a little caller ID modem, a DEC-
talk and some simple programming can curtail the over-zealous ringing of the 
telephone.

