There were two different versions of the ECU (EISA Configuration Utility)
diskette.

One version was for setting up a system for use with OpenVMS or OSF /
DIGITAL UNIX / Tru64 UNIX. The other version of the diskette was for setting
up a system for use with Microsofts Windows NT.

OpenVMS and UNIX cannot share interupts, Windows NT can share interupts. The
difference between these two diskettes is the *.CFG / Configuration files.

All Alpha system managers should run the proper ECU once, prior to the
installation of the Operating System. The ECU initializes the built-in
hardware on the system properly.

A major customer recently had a problem with AlphaServer 4100 systems, the
floppy disk drive worked at the console level, it tested out fine and even
worked with Windows NT, but when Tru64 UNIX was installed, the floppy disk
drive was not visible to UNIX. Hilariously, Compaq sent a Service Tech to a
few sites with this problem, and a swapping of hardware was started, they
swapped the floppy disk drives, the PCI backplane, the main system
motherboard interconnecting  the memory and CPU's and cables and power
supplies and nothing worked. Then someone actually ran the proper ECU for
Tru64 UNIX and lo and behold, the floppy disk drive works properly with
UNIX. It seems that my standing orders in the specific manufacturing site
for these systems had been forgotten, that was, always run the proper ECU,
whether or not you think it needs it. It won't harm anything and it will
resolve a multitude of numerous problems, unexplainable any other way.

Well, now that Compaq has closed most all of the DIGITAL manufacturing sites
and let go almost anyone who ever knew anything about Alpha's in
manufacturing, the standard default stuff that we used to do in
manufacturing has been forgotten or not passed on. So, for example, we used
to set up the Environmental Variables in the console in manufacturing. This
is no longer done, so a system manager should verify these settings
properly, prior to installing the Operating System.


