In article <5vgK6.1838$A07.406165@homer.alpha.net>, "matt anderson" <manderson@nospam.mattand.com> writes:

  Please remember to pick a title relevent to the question...

:...trying to... get... AlphaStation 600...up and running....
:They don't have graphics cards and I believe rely on tty's for console
:access.

  Do you know if these systems were working in recent history, and do 
  you know if any of the component modules may have been removed (or
  added) since the systems were last functional?

  AlphaStation systems were sold with graphics cards, so I will assume
  that the cards have been removed.  The SRM console -- used for Tru64 
  UNIX and OpenVMS -- would thus be willing to use the serial port.  If 
  you have the AlphaBIOS (ARC) console required by Windows NT, you will
  want to follow the instructions in the OpenVMS FAQ for switching to
  the SRM console.

  You will want to see exactly what lurks in the PCI...

  If you DO have a graphics card, you will want to identify the
  specific card visually, or via the console.  (Once you get into
  the console, of course.)

:When they power up, the front LCD's say "srom 1.2 XX" and XX counts down a
:bit and finally settles on "cd" (not sure if that's even related to compact
:disc).

  CD would be the diagnostic code.  In the case of the AlphaStation
  600 series, CD indicates that the SROM code execution is complete.

:First, what kind of serial console should I be using? I'm trying to watch
:serial port 1 with hyperterminal and a crossover serial cable and never see
:anything. Also don't see anything with a straight thru cable.

  Information on the necessary cabling and pinouts is listed in the
  OpenVMS FAQ.

:Second - from the LCD readout, is the system even getting far enough that I
:*should* see anything?

  Yes, it appears you should be able to access the system via the
  serial console.

  Try a couple of CTRL/P characters or a BREAK on COM1, and see if 
  you can get the attention of the console program.  (The console 
  environment variable CONSOLE -- the mechanism that selects the 
  workstation or COM1 serial console -- may be set to GRAPHICS, not 
  SERIAL...)

  With the I/O adapters to the left and the SIMM slots to the bottom, 
  there will be a clump of eight jumpers on the extreme right margin 
  of the motherboard.  The third jumper down from the top (W3) selects 
  the failsafe firmware loader.  Details on the firmware failsafe loader 
  operation for various systems (though not the AlphaStation 600) are 
  available at the Ask The Wizard website, and at the Alpha firmware 
  website.  See the OpenVMS FAQ for pointers to both.

  You can use the failsafe loader to load current firmware into the box,
  if the firmware should be corrupted or of an antique version.

:Thanks a bunch. Google groups ( I miss Deja!) didn't yield much useful, and
:compaq's website is useless.

  The OpenVMS FAQ -- available at the Compaq OpenVMS website -- has the
  information that will be of interest to you.


