In article <lcSK6.3076$RX4.218432@news.infostrada.it>, "Paolo" <zupaolo@NOSP@Mlibero.it> writes:
>    I am in the process to buy a Digital Personal Workstation and
>I was wondering what are the differences between the a and au
>series. I have seen that a's can run only WinNT while au's can run
>DGUX and openvms. Is there something different in the hardware?
>From the specs I could not figure out it.

No, not DGUX. Data General's UNIX on Digital's Alpha Hardware ?
It's Tru64 UNIX (formerly known as Digital UNIX formerly known as DEC OSF/1)

Basically there is no difference between a PWS a and au,
except:

1) For Tru64 and OpenVMS you need/run the SRM firmware, and for
Windows-NT you used to use the AlphaBIOS (also known as ARC on other
alpha hardware models). Both firmware versions reside in a flash chip,
which is (usually) big enough to contain both versions on the same time.
So, all you need to do is switch over to the SRM console in case you run
the AlphaBIOS Firmware.

btw: Check the firmware versions. I bet there are already newer ones
to can/should load into the flash:

eg.	ftp://ftp.digital.com/pub/digital/Alpha/firmware/index.html


2) the widgets (graphic controller, disk controller, ...) are different

As OpenVMS supports much fewer controllers than Tru64 UNIX which supports
much fewer controllers than Windows-NT used to support, you may end up
with replacing all your PCI boards with other ones which are supported
by the opsys you want to run on. Because the NT supported so much controllers,
"A" models were equipped with (almost) the cheapest available cards, just
to keep competitive with other low-cost-WINTEL boxes (which never came true)

There is no processor/motherboard difference for A and AU.
I think this all is discussed in the VMS FAQ.
Better get it and jump into as soon as possible...

