  TGV grew out of software developed at SRI. They mostly did VMS TCP/IP,
though there were odds-n-ends (they bought out UUNET's share of BSDI, for
example).

  TGV sold the company and products to Cisco, in one of the Cisco acquis-
ition binges. Not that much later, Cisco sold the TCP/IP product to Process
Software. I don't know where the part-ownership of BSDI went, and Cisco
kept the X.25 gateway software, which they'd previously licensed for IOS
from TGV.

  Process Software then made a not-entirely-successful attempt to get into
high-priced add-on software (the IPWorks family) and eventually split into
separate companies. The Process Software side was acquired by Platinum
Equity Holdings and continues operation (and development/support of the
non-IPWorks products like MultiNet). IPWorks (according to their web site)
laid off their entire staff in September, 2000.

  Meanwhile, some of the original TGV folks surfaced at Network Alchemy,
but they were acquired by Nokia in February, 2000 (see the press release
at http://press.nokia.com/PR/200002/775433_5.html).

  Ken Adelman's [TGV co-founder] web page (http://www.adelman.org) says
he's retired, but gives an email address for him.

  Dave Kashtan's [likewise] web page is at http://www.kashtan.com.

  This is probably more than you ever wanted to know, but hopefully it'll
be useful.

