In article <3AEF2D49.4EA95C7E@gsfc.nasa.gov>, Robert DiRosario <robert.j.dirosario.1@gsfc.nasa.gov> writes:
:Does it have ethernet and DSSI or SCSI access without using the Qbus?  

  Ethernet, yes.   DSSI or SCSI, no -- you would need to connect via
  Ethernet or via FQAM (or less preferable, via FTAM) and the Q-bus.

  The only M-bus I/O widgets that I can recall are the Q-bus adapters
  (the FTAM and FQAM) and the VCB03 graphics controller.

:I've heard from several people that using ethernet, DSSI or SCSI over a 
:Qbus is very slow.

  You do clearly realize the, um, relative absurdity of discussing the 
  performance of (circa) dozen-year-old-hardware, of course.  :-)

  Ethernet (the ten megabit "classic" variant), SCSI-1, and DSSI (DSSI 
  is roughly the same speed as SCSI-1) are all very slow. 

  The Q-bus is itself arguably somewhat faster than these widgets.

  The VAXstation 3520 and VAXstation 3540 single-stream performance is 
  CVAX-speed, roughly 2.8 VUPS, plus whatever performance boost you can 
  obtain by keeping the secondary processors busy.  Slow, in other words.

  If you were to ask my opinion for a hobbyist VAX system, I'd point you at 
  a VAXstation 4000 model 60 or model 90 or such -- these boxes are among 
  the fastest VAX systems, are far smaller than the VAXstation 3520, and 
  have "amenities" including integrated SCSI and Ethernet, etc...  For
  a hobbyist Alpha platform, most any AlphaStation box...  (The lure of
  an SMP-capable VAXstation system simply isn't large enough. :-)


