In article <3AEF2D49.4EA95C7E@gsfc.nasa.gov>, Robert DiRosario <robert.j.dirosario.1@gsfc.nasa.gov> writes:
> Does it have ethernet and DSSI or SCSI access without using the Qbus?  I've heard from
> several people that using ethernet, DSSI or SCSI over a Qbus is very slow.

Yes, it does. The minimal Firefox is a CPU, a memory card, and an I/O board.
The I/O board contains DSSI, Ethernet, and the serial ports for console,
printer, mouse, and keyboard. There's an additional three-board set for
video (which I yanked from mine because I don't have a monitor) and,
of course, the ever popular Qbus adapter options. The only other Mbus
device (AFAIK) was the original MasPar Front-End InterFace, which was
an adapter to a modified VMEbus (essentially, VMEbus with parity across
the cable between the Firefox and the MasPar).
