In article <9CFH6.8$lP5.137@news.cpqcorp.net>, hoffman@xdelta.zko.dec.nospam (Hoff Hoffman) writes:
>   Correct.  The early adapter was the FTAM/L2008, and supported only TQK70 
>   due to slow(er) slave read data signaling.  The later DWFQA/FQAM/L2002 
>   widget supported specific other Q-bus widgets.  (I have a recollection
>   that programmed I/O was required, and DMA Q-bus widgets were not useful.
>   But it's been a long time since I've worked with a Firefox box...)

I designed FQAM, so I can tell you all about it.

The FTAM was the original simple-minded idea to hook CQBIC up to the
FBIC to provide QBus. The theory was that FBIC provides the CVAX pin-bus
and CQBIC takes CVAX pin-bus and creates QBus. Nice theory, but shot in
the foot by the timing of write-back transactions on the M-bus.

FQAM tightly controlled the M-bus to be able to guarantee the QBus DMA
timing, but as a result performance really really sucked. It worked, but
it could only do about 250KB/s DMA throughput and while it was doing
DMA _nothing_ else happened on the system (if FQAM allowed anything else
to happen, it could no longer guarantee that it could meet DMA timing
requirements).

So, DMA works with FQAM, but is painful. TQK70 worked with FTAM because it
waited around 20 microseconds for a DMA transaction to happen. I have a
modified RQDX3 which also works with FTAM (it waits forever for the
transaction to complete), but the modification wasn't realistic for
shipment.

My original concept for a working QBus adapter for Firefox involved a
shared memory between Mbus and Qbus; device drivers would spoon-feed data
into the shared memory and DMA devices would DMA it out. I made the
mistake of trying to sell it to the VMS folks by saying "it's just like
the MicroVAX I". Their memories of MicroVAX I were just too painful...
